# Java Generics

## What are Generics?

Generics allow a class or collection to work with a **specific type** while providing compile-time type safety.

### Without Generics

```java
List list = new ArrayList();

list.add(10);
list.add("Hello");

Integer x = (Integer) list.get(1); // Runtime error
With Generics
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add("Hello"); // ❌ Compile-time error

Integer x = list.get(0);

Main benefit: Type safety + no unnecessary casting.

Generic Class

T is a type parameter (a placeholder for a type).

class Box<T> {

    private T value;

    void set(T value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    T get() {
        return value;
    }
}

Usage:

Box<Integer> box = new Box<>();

box.set(10);

Integer value = box.get();

Here:

T = Integer

We can reuse the same class:

Box<String> box = new Box<>();

box.set("Hello");

Here:

T = String
Multiple Type Parameters
class Pair<K, V> {

    K key;
    V value;
}

Usage:

Pair<String, Integer> pair = new Pair<>();

Here:

K = String
V = Integer

This is the same idea used by:

Map<String, User>
K = String
V = User
LLD Example

A generic Cache:

class Cache<K, V> {

    V get(K key) {
        // ...
        return null;
    }

    void put(K key, V value) {
        // ...
    }
}

Now the same class can be used for:

Cache<String, User>
Cache<String, Movie>
Cache<String, Booking>
Remember

Generics = type placeholders that make code reusable and type-safe.

T  → one type
K,V → two types